+`docs/adr/ADR-003-rabbitmq.md`:
+```markdown
+# ADR-003: Why RabbitMQ
+
+## Status
+Accepted
+
+## Context
+We need a message broker for asynchronous communication between microservices. The broker must support persistent messages, flexible routing, and dead letter queues.
+
+## Decision
+Use RabbitMQ as the message broker.
+
+## Rationale
+- **Persistent messages**: Survives broker restarts
+- **Flexible routing**: Direct, topic, fanout, header exchanges
+- **Dead letter queues**: Automatic DLQ for failed messages
+- **Consumer acknowledgments**: at-least-once delivery semantics
+- **Management UI**: Built-in monitoring and management
+- **Protocol**: AMQP 0-9-1 is a mature, well-documented protocol
+- **Performance**: Handles tens of thousands of messages per second
+
+## Consequences
+- Stateful broker requires careful operational management
+- Message ordering not guaranteed across queues
+- AMQP adds protocol overhead compared to in-process communication
+```
+
